Stefan Palme wrote: > Hi, > > after reading another recent thread on this list I have question: > > Wietse wrote: > >> Virtual domains are not the correct solution for this. >> >> For mail that is delivered elsewhere use relay_domains and >> relay_recipient_maps, and perhaps transport_maps. >> > > I have a setup running postfix and an IMAP server on the same host. > Several domains are configured using virtual_alias_domains and > virtual_alias_maps (see postconf -n at the end). Transport to > the local IMAP server happens using LMTP. > > Although the IMAP server is obviously not "remote", LMTP is a > "remote" protocol, so for this setup Wietse's statement would > apply, too (because "mail ist delivered elsewhere"). > Does it mean, I better should use relay_domains for this setup? > > If the answer is "yes" - how can I realize all the virtual alias > mappings currently done in virtual_alias_maps? > > The IMAP server uses an LDAP server for authenticating users, > so the unix accounts on the host and the valid IMAP accounts are > totally independent from each other (maybe THIS is the definition > of "remote" Wietse means?) > the problem is that many people confuse virtual_alias_maps and virtual_alias_domains - virtual_alias_maps apply to _all_ mail, whatever is the domain class. - virtual_alias_domains is for "alias" domains. In general, users in these domains must be redirected to addresses in other domains, or routed to some transport that knows how to handle such addresses. Other than that, domain classes are not a "strict" concept. what you need to know is that postfix has convenient defaults (such as delivering to a mailbox) but in some cases, your judgement is needed to find the most appropriate class. sometimes, it doesn't matter. you must be aware of the following things. - relay control. mail is accepted from public clients if it is for a domain that is in mydestination, relay_domains, virtual_mailbox_domains or virtual_alias_domains. all othere mail is rejected by reject_unath_destination. This is the only reason why alias domains are listed in virtual_alias_domains.* *- address validation. you must validate addresses when accepting mail (to avoid bouncing and useless work). depending on the address class, different maps are used to validate addresses (local_recipient_maps for mydestination, ... etc). - mail routing. postfix has defaults to deliver to each domain class: local domains are delivered via local, virtual_mailbox via virtual, relay_domains via relay, virtual_alias_domains mail is rewritten to other domains... You can override the default behaviour using transport_maps, whenever this is appropriate (this must still be subjected to the usual rule: don't fight against the system. Instead, find out how to make the system do what you want, and stay in peace).